      "math_academy_id": "e-18930",
      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}} &= \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}} &= \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}) }{(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x})(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})} &= \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}) }{(\\sqrt{2})^2-(\\sqrt{x})^2} &= \\[5pt]\n\\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}) }{2-x} \n\t}\n$$",
